# The journal — `docs/plan/` and `docs/CHANGELOG.md` — records how *this*
# repository got here and what it shipped (ADR-0065 §3, the starter lifecycle,
# as carried by ADR-0081 §5 and ADR-0082 §4). Two kinds of merge meet it, and
# the attributes serve both without a driver defined anywhere:
#
# - A lane merge — a lane branch and `main`, in the starter or in a fork. The
#   notes and the changelog have one writer each and are only appended to, so
#   `union` keeps both lanes' entries. The ledger, the plans and the lane files
#   are written by more than one lane, and keep git's ordinary merge: `ours`
#   names a driver no clone defines, so git falls back to the text merge, and a
#   collision conflicts rather than dropping a side.
# - The starter merge — `starter/main` into a fork. The fork's journal is kept
#   whole: the command defines both drivers for itself alone, `ours` for the
#   ledger and the plans and `union` for the notes and the changelog, each as
#   `true`, which keeps the fork's side of every file both sides changed (git
#   looks a driver up in the config before its own built-in `union`):
#
#     git -c merge.ours.driver=true -c merge.union.driver=true merge starter/main
#
# Never define either driver in a clone's or a global git config: with
# `merge.ours.driver` every lane merge would keep its own ledger, and with
# `merge.union.driver` its own notes and changelog, silently dropping the other
# lane's. Union keeps both sides of a collision without a conflict, so the
# changelog's preamble and a notes file's existing text are edited only by the
# lane that ships; `pnpm docs:check` refuses the duplicated headings a union of
# two edits leaves.
# `scripts/merge-attributes.test.mjs` proves both merges against this file.
docs/plan/** merge=ours
docs/plan/*.notes.md merge=union
docs/CHANGELOG.md merge=union

# `scripts/rules-digest.mjs` writes these with `endOfLine: "lf"` and compares the
# result byte for byte against what is on disk. Under `core.autocrlf=true` a fresh
# clone would check them out as CRLF, and the generator would call every digest
# stale on a tree nobody had touched. `CLAUDE.md` is measured in bytes against a
# budget, so it is pinned for the same reason.
docs/rules/** text eol=lf
CLAUDE.md text eol=lf

# The migration runner hashes each file as committed and records that hash
# (ADR-0078 §1, applied by hash). Under `core.autocrlf=true` a checkout would
# rewrite every line ending and change every hash; pinned to LF, the bytes on
# disk are the bytes in the repository on every machine. The runner also
# normalises before hashing — this is what keeps the file itself honest.
packages/db/migrations/** text eol=lf
